  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-121931",
    "knowledge_point_id": "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector",
    "problem": "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4t-{t}^{3},2{t}^{2}+t⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$",
    "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{t}^{3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4-3{t}^{2},4t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-3{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6t,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
